@* The sidebar's Administration group: one link, to the Users administration
   page (issue #28). Shared by the dashboard and that page rather than
   written twice, because the thing worth keeping in one place is not the
   markup but who gets to see it.

   Inside an AuthorizeView on the Administrator policy, so a User without the
   capability is shown no link to a page that would refuse them - the policy
   on the page itself is what actually refuses (AdministratorRequirement),
   and this is the half that keeps the sidebar honest.

   A plain <a href>, like the rest of the sidebar: changing section is a
   navigation the browser performs, not a circuit message (ADR 0003). *@

<AuthorizeView Policy="@Constants.Authorization.Administrator">
    <Authorized>
        <p class="hub-sidebar-group hub-section-label">@Constants.Users.NavGroupAdministration</p>

        <a class="@NavItemClass" href="@AppRoutes.Users" aria-current="@AriaCurrent">
            <HubIcon Name="@Constants.Icons.Users" Size="@Constants.Icons.SidebarSize" />
            <span>@Constants.Users.NavUsersLabel</span>
        </a>
    </Authorized>
</AuthorizeView>

@code {
    [Parameter]
    public bool Current { get; set; }

    private string NavItemClass => Current ? "hub-nav-item current" : "hub-nav-item";

    // Null renders no attribute at all, which is what "not the current page"
    // has to be: aria-current="false" is a value.
    private string? AriaCurrent => Current ? "page" : null;
}
